# Files this preset must never score as this repository's own prose.
#
# textlint auto-detects this file (see `textlint --help`'s `--ignore-path` default), so every
# invocation honours it: `vp check`, an ad hoc `node_modules/.bin/textlint`, a future CI step,
# and `scripts/ci/check-dogfood-lint.mjs`. That is the point of putting the exclusion here rather
# than inside one script's target globs — a glob only protects the tool that reads it, and the
# dogfood ratchet is not the only thing that runs textlint against this repository.
#
# Two categories belong here, and nothing else:
#
#   1. Content whose violations are the deliverable, not debt. `examples/sample.md` and
#      `examples/rule-pack/sample.md` are each documented in their own README.md as carrying
#      "deliberate violations" — the whole point of the file is to fail the linter so a
#      first-time user sees real findings. Scoring one as prose debt would be scoring a fixture
#      against the thing it exists to demonstrate.
#
#   2. Content this repository does not author. `fixtures/original/` and `fixtures/compliant/`
#      are verbatim (or licensed-rewrite) excerpts of other projects' documentation, tracked for
#      provenance in fixtures/manifest.json and fixtures/LICENSES.md — see docs/fixtures.md. They
#      are the corpus the rule engine is tested against, not writing this project is responsible
#      for. Neither directory is in scripts/ci/check-dogfood-lint.mjs's target globs today, but
#      excluding them here means a broadened glob, or any future ad hoc scan, cannot silently
#      start treating a licensed excerpt as this repository's own lint debt.
#
# A file belongs on this list only for one of those two reasons. "It currently has a lot of
# errors" is not one of them — that is what scripts/ci/dogfood-lint-baseline.json is for.

examples/sample.md
examples/rule-pack/sample.md
fixtures/original/**
fixtures/compliant/**
